@startuml domain-published-language
' Report §2.4 / §3.5 — the Published Language: every event record in common-events, its
' payload and its Kafka key. Source: include/domain-published-language.iuml.
!pragma layout smetana
!include include/style.iuml
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
hide empty members

title Published Language (common-events) — domain events

!include include/domain-published-language.iuml

EventPublished ..|> DomainEvent
SessionAdded ..|> DomainEvent
SessionCapacityChanged ..|> DomainEvent
RegistrationRequested ..|> DomainEvent
SeatsReserved ..|> DomainEvent
SeatsReleased ..|> DomainEvent
RegistrationConfirmed ..|> DomainEvent
RegistrationCancelled ..|> DomainEvent
RegistrationExpired ..|> DomainEvent
RefundRequested ..|> DomainEvent
WaitlistJoined ..|> DomainEvent
WaitlistPromoted ..|> DomainEvent
PaymentSucceeded ..|> DomainEvent
PaymentFailed ..|> DomainEvent
RefundIssued ..|> DomainEvent
RefundDeclined ..|> DomainEvent
TicketIssued ..|> DomainEvent
TicketTransferred ..|> DomainEvent
TicketVoided ..|> DomainEvent
AttendeeCheckedIn ..|> DomainEvent

note top of DomainEvent
  Topic = eventType(): the record's simple name in kebab-case,
  so SessionCapacityChanged → session-capacity-changed.
  Key = partitionKey(): every record overrides it (shown as
  "key:" on each record). Records carry data only — no Spring,
  no JPA, no behaviour beyond derived accessors.
end note

legend right
  The key choice is deliberate: the seat topics and
  session-capacity-changed share sessionId so the RT1
  fill-rate join needs no repartition; the saga topics
  share registrationId so one registration's messages
  stay in order; the ticket topics use ticketCode.
  AttendeeCheckedIn rejects checkedInAt < queuedAt;
  RegistrationConfirmed rejects an empty sessionIds.
endlegend
@enduml
